SONOFF RF Bridge R2 — IoTCloud v2
================================

This project receives stock iTead RF UART messages and publishes events only for
registered RF codes. It targets the ESP8285 with 1 MB flash, the stock RF MCU,
UART0 RX/TX on GPIO3/GPIO1 at 19200 8N1, button GPIO0, and status LED GPIO13.
It does not require replacing the RF MCU firmware or rewiring its receiver.
Confirm the flash size/board revision before flashing. Standard stock-MCU RF
transmission is supported; Portisch raw/bucket modes, rolling codes, and
unsupported RF protocols remain outside this implementation.

Build and install
-----------------

From the repository root:

    pio run -d IotCloud_sonoff_rf
    pio run -d IotCloud_sonoff_rf -t upload --upload-port <serial-port>

If pio is not in PATH, use ~/.platformio/penv/bin/pio.
The source now lives under src/, following the other PlatformIO projects.
Dependencies and the ESP8285 baseline come from ../common_env.ini; model/version
are defined in platformio.ini and injected by the shared OTA build helper.

Use a serial flash for the initial migration. The image reserves 64 KB for
LittleFS at 0xEB000..0xFB000, with the separate IoTCloud EEPROM sector at 0xFB000.
A previous firmware with a different flash layout may require provisioning again.
No filesystem image upload is required. Avoid uploadfs: it replaces the registry.

The UART is exclusively for RF: application/library serial logging is disabled.
A serial monitor will show binary RF frames, not a text console. Disconnect the
USB UART transmitter from the shared RX line during normal operation.

OTA
---

This project uses the same shared `upload_remote` target as the other devices.
Its `custom_ota_compress = yes` setting makes that helper gzip the image before
uploading it. Compression is needed because the roughly 550 KB firmware plus a
second uncompressed copy does not fit below the 64 KB LittleFS partition on a
1 MB chip. The other 1 MB builds are smaller and have no filesystem partition,
so their uncompressed images fit.

After installing and validating this version on hardware, future model-wide
releases can use:

    pio run -d IotCloud_sonoff_rf -t upload_remote

That target publishes a release for ALL SONOFF_RF devices through the existing
IoTCloud deployment helper; it is not a single-device serial upload. The OTA
server must serve the actual gzip bytes, without HTTP content decompression.
Compressed OTA needs the gzip-capable ESP8266 eboot included by the current core,
installed with the initial serial flash. No upload or release deployment is
performed by a normal build.

MQTT topics
-----------

Use the existing device prefix:

    BASE = v1/<locationId>/<deviceId>/
    RF = BASE<deviceInternalId>_001_RF/aux/rf/

Normal clients send commands through the IoTCloud REST API; the Rust gateway
publishes the resulting MQTT messages. User MQTT tokens are read-only. For broker
administration and firmware diagnostics, subscribe to `RF#` before sending raw
commands. Commands are JSON and must be published with retain=false.

Every command includes a unique `request_id` (1–64 characters). Results echo it.
The `request_id` returned by a successful learn command is also its
`learning_session_id`.

Command          Additional payload
initialize       "confirm":"ERASE_RF_REGISTRY"
learn            starts a 60-second capture window
register         "learning_session_id", "id", "code", optional "message", "repeats", "debounce_ms"
cancel           "learning_session_id"
delete           "id"
send             "sync", "low", "high", "code"
list             {}

Output       Meaning
status       Retained boot/protocol/registry identity, storage state, learning flag, counters; published on state change
result       Non-retained command result
candidate    Non-retained code/timings, readings, ready
devices      Non-retained records with index/total, then complete:true
event        Non-retained registered event: boot ID, sequence, id/message/code/timings/uptime/occurred_at

All output contains `protocol_version:1` and a per-boot `boot_id`. Registry
fragments contain a common `snapshot_id` and `registry_revision`; consumers must
replace their cache only after receiving all indexed fragments and the matching
`complete:true` marker.

`send` transmits a standard stock-MCU A5 code. A successful result reports
`"result":"sent"`, `"delivery":"rf_mcu_uart"`, and
`"receiver_confirmed":false`: all bytes were written and flushed to the RF MCU,
but the firmware cannot confirm that another device received or acted on the RF
signal. Sending is rejected while learning is active. A matching A4 frame
received within one second after a
send is treated as the bridge's own echo, counted as `suppressed`, and is not
eligible for learning or event publication. This can also suppress a genuine
matching remote pressed during that one-second interval.

Example command:

    Topic: RFsend
    Payload: {"request_id":"example-send-1","sync":8192,"low":300,"high":900,"code":1906529}

Initialize storage once
-----------------------

Mounting never automatically formats flash. If `RFstatus` reports "unmounted",
initialize the reserved filesystem explicitly:

Topic: RFinitialize
Payload: {"request_id":"example-initialize-1","confirm":"ERASE_RF_REGISTRY"}

This erases ALL RF registrations in the LittleFS partition. It does not erase
IoTCloud's separate EEPROM configuration. Do not initialize a working registry.
If an existing registry is corrupt, status reports "invalid_registry", events
are disabled, and writes are refused. Initialization is also an explicit recovery
option, after saving/recovering any registrations you need.

Register the door
-----------------

1. Start capture:

   Topic: RFlearn
   Payload: {"request_id":"learn-front-door-1"}

2. Trigger the intended door sensor several times. Observe `RFcandidate`.
   A candidate becomes ready after at least three readings with the same code
   and pulse timings within 25% of the first reading. Up to eight distinct codes
   are collected per window. Each candidate is published for its first three
   matching readings, limiting traffic from chatty transmitters. If the window
   fills with other devices, cancel and restart nearer the intended sensor.
   Capture does not save or automatically choose the first signal.

3. Within 60 seconds, explicitly select the observed code:

   Topic: RFregister
   Payload: {"request_id":"register-front-door-1","learning_session_id":"learn-front-door-1","id":"front_door_open","code":1906529,"message":"Puerta de casa abierta","repeats":2,"debounce_ms":3000}

   1906529 is the old firmware's code, shown only as an example. Use it only if
   the new capture confirms it. The registry starts empty; there is no implicit
   migration of the old hardcoded code without verified timing data.

4. Wait for result "registered", then request `RFlist` and verify the record.
   Registration ends capture. Reboot and request `RFlist` to check persistence.
   Test both the door and unrelated remotes before using its events.

There are at most 5 records. IDs contain 1–32 ASCII letters, digits, underscores,
or hyphens. Codes are JSON decimal integers, 0–16777215 (not hex strings).
Messages are at most 96 UTF-8 bytes and are included as optional display metadata
in the structured RF event. Defaults: message=id, repeats=2, debounce_ms=3000.
Repeats must be 1–5; debounce_ms must be 100–60000.

Registering the same ID after a fresh capture replaces it. A code cannot belong
to two IDs. Register distinct signals (open/closed/buttons) as distinct IDs; the
bridge publishes events, not an inferred door state. To delete a record:

    {"request_id":"delete-front-door-1","id":"front_door_open"}

Publish that payload to `RFdelete`. Removing the last record persists an
empty registry. Writes use a verified temporary JSON file and an atomic LittleFS
rename. Failed writes leave the previous RAM and committed file configuration
active. The JSON schema version is 1 and the path is /rf-devices.json.

RF matching and event delivery
------------------------------

Frames must have a valid start/action/fixed length/trailer. AA/55 in payload bytes
are preserved; partial frames expire after a 100 ms gap, and malformed streams
resynchronize without writing outside the parser buffer. Stock A2/A3/A4 messages
receive an AA A0 55 acknowledgement. ACKs are not acknowledged again. Only A4
receptions enter event matching. Learning is a software capture window, not the
RF MCU's A1 learning command, so normal reception continues.

An event requires an exact 24-bit code AND sync/low/high times within 25% of the
registered reference. Confirmation requires the configured repeat count within
one second, with no gap above 500 ms. Each record has its own confirmation and
3000 ms default debounce; unknown codes and unrelated activity cannot suppress it.
A held transmitter can produce one event per debounce period.

Some RF MCU/sensor combinations report just one UART frame per activation. If
capture confirms that behavior, re-register with repeats=1. Missing radio signals
cannot be reconstructed. This stock protocol has no checksum or authenticated
sender identity: another transmitter with the same code and timing is inherently
indistinguishable. Timing matching/repeat confirmation reduce false triggers but
do not add RF authentication.

The RF bridge exports sensor suffix `001_RF` and sensor type `rf_bridge`.
Registered events are published only to the structured RF event topic:

    BASE<deviceInternalId>_001_RF/aux/rf/event

Events are non-retained, QoS 0, and only published while connected. There is no
offline event queue or delivery guarantee; counters are diagnostic and reset on
reboot. Provisioning/reset of IoTCloud settings does not clear the RF registry;
use `RFdelete` or explicit `RFinitialize` to remove registrations.

Validation
----------

    sh IotCloud_sonoff_rf/test/run.sh

Run after the PlatformIO build has installed ArduinoJson. Host tests exercise the
actual parser/filter and bridge implementation, using in-memory serial, MQTT, and
filesystem adapters. Address/undefined-behavior sanitizers cover binary marker
bytes, corruption/noise, timeouts, random byte streams, debounce/wraparound,
registration confirmation, invalid commands, reboot recovery, failed writes and
renames, deletion, reconnect handling, and corrupt-registry recovery. Hardware UART,
real flash power-loss behavior, RF reception, MQTT broker ACLs, and OTA deployment
still need validation on the physical unit.

